;; symbol-namespace-vocabulary.wat — the naming target for arc 251's SYMBOL correction.
;;
;; ── THE CORRECTION BEING NAMED ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;
;; wat has never implemented symbols. It has a `WatAST::Keyword(String, Span)` whose own doc
;; comment admits the fusion, verbatim from crates/wat-reader/src/ast.rs:99-107:
;;
;;   "Keyword token, as in `:foo`, `:wat::holon::Atom` ... Used both as keyword literals
;;    (payloads for wat keyword atoms) AND as keyword-path references (heads of calls, type
;;    annotations). Distinguished by context at later passes."
;;
;; That last sentence is the defect, written at the definition site. One node does two
;; unrelated jobs — a VALUE and a REFERENCE — and every downstream pass must re-decide which.
;;
;; The builder's ruling (2026-08-13): `:wat::core::+` NEVER WAS A KEYWORD. It is a
;; COLON-QUOTED SYMBOL, misfiled as a keyword since the beginning. The correction is not
;; "add symbols" — it is un-misfiling the ones we always had.
;;
;; ── THE SHAPE RULED ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;
;;   every symbol is (namespace, name, scopes) — a namespace is NEVER absent
;;
;;   <binder-ns>/x    a LOCAL binder   — reserved, un-spellable by user source
;;   wat.core/+       a reference      — the substrate's
;;   user/f           a reference      — the program's
;;   :foo             a KEYWORD, and only ever a value
;;
;; The rule this makes structural (today it holds, but only at RUNTIME — a bare `(foo 1)`
;; head fails with UnboundSymbol at eval, never at check):
;;
;;   A NON-NAMESPACED SYMBOL IS LEGAL ONLY IN A BINDER POSITION.
;;   ALL OTHER SYMBOLS MUST BE NAMESPACED.
;;
;; Giving binders their own reserved namespace makes every symbol ONE shape, so "is this a
;; reference?" becomes a namespace match instead of the four hand-rolled string tests that
;; decide it today, each independently:
;;
;;   src/resolve/normalize.rs:81   ident.as_str().contains('/')
;;   src/macros/expand.rs:537      ident.as_str().contains('/')
;;   src/runtime.rs:3048           s.as_str().contains('/')
;;   src/runtime.rs:3307           s.as_str().contains('/')
;;
;; ── WHY A NAMED NAMESPACE AND NOT `(Option :- [Namespace])` ───────────────────────────────
;;
;; The alternative shape is an optional namespace whose `None` means "this is a binder".
;; That was cut, on precedent one day old (arc 278 R66): an `Option` whose `None` meant
;; "skip the check" collapsed two unrelated situations and passed BOTH. A named namespace
;; makes the binder case a match arm that cannot be forgotten; `None` is what gets
;; `unwrap_or_default`'d. Uniform shape > optional field.
;;
;; ── GROUNDING FOR THE NAMESPACE SPELLING ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;
;; `$`-prefixed namespaces are LEGAL IN CLOJURE — verified by the builder against Clojure
;; 1.12.4 in a live REPL:
;;
;;   user=>   (ns $bound)          ;; nil
;;   $bound=> (def x [42])         ;; #'$bound/x
;;   $bound=> (ns user)            ;; nil
;;   user=>   (println $bound/x)   ;; [42]
;;
;; So a `$`-prefixed namespace is Clojure-faithful, not a wat-only hack — which matters,
;; because wat is a Clojure DIALECT (300 R7 VIRTVTE PARES).
;;
;; MEASURED FREE: zero occurrences of `$bound` or `$binder` as a namespace anywhere in
;; wat/, wat-tests/, tests/, wat-scripts/, src/, crates/. Probed live: `$binder/x` in head
;; position resolves to `:$binder::x` and reports UnresolvedReference — nobody owns it.
;; The `$` character already lexes in identifiers (`$x` is a legal binder today), so the
;; reader needs NO change to carry it.
;;
;; RESERVATION MECHANISM (exists, one door): src/resolve/reserved.rs
;;   pub const RESERVED_PREFIXES: &[&str] = &[":wat::", ":rust::"];
;;   pub fn is_reserved_prefix(keyword: &str) -> bool
;; already consulted at registration, resolution, and closure extraction. The binder
;; namespace joins that list as one entry, so user source cannot define into it.

;; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; SLOT 1 — THE RESERVED BINDER NAMESPACE
;; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;;
;; The namespace every non-namespaced (binder) symbol silently carries. It appears in NO
;; user source — `(let [x 1] ... x)` is unchanged. It is what you get back when you ASK a
;; symbol for its namespace, and what an error message says when a bare symbol appears
;; somewhere a reference was required.
;;
;; The two the builder offered, plus siblings weighed for register:
;;
;;   $bound    — "this name is BOUND here" — the state of the thing. Reads as a fact about
;;               the symbol (it has a binding in scope). Clojure-verified spelling above.
;;   $binder   — "this name is a BINDER" — the ROLE. But a binder is the thing that BINDS
;;               (the `[x 1]` slot); a USE of `x` in the body is a bound OCCURRENCE, not a
;;               binder. Does the name mislead at the use site, which is most sites?
;;   $local    — the scoping fact: not global, not namespaced, resolved in the frame.
;;               Familiar from every language's "local variable". Says less about binding.
;;   $lexical  — precise (lexical scope) but heavier; does it earn the extra syllable?
;;   $scope    — names the mechanism (`Identifier` already carries `scopes: BTreeSet<ScopeId>`
;;               for hygiene), but then the namespace and the scope-set say the same word for
;;               two different things — a collision of vocabulary, not of names.
;;
;; ★ THE DISCRIMINATOR TO WEIGH: the namespace is read at the USE site far more often than
;;   at the binding site. Which spelling keeps its promise when the reader meets it in an
;;   error message about `x` appearing where `wat.core/foo` was required?

(:wat::core::defn :naming::SLOT-1-binder-namespace
  [] -> :wat::core::String
  ;; What a symbol's namespace accessor returns for a plain `x` bound by a `let`.
  ;; <binder-ns-name> goes here — the reserved namespace, e.g. "$bound".
  "PLACEHOLDER-BINDER-NAMESPACE")

;; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; SLOT 2 — THE ACCESSOR THAT ASKS A SYMBOL FOR ITS NAMESPACE
;; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;;
;; Given a symbol, yield the namespace part. Total — every symbol has one, which is the
;; whole point of the correction (no Option, no absence).
;;
;; SIBLINGS it sits beside (the wat surface's existing reflection/accessor tone):
;;   (:wat::core::ast-kind node)      ;; -> String   the node's kind
;;   (:wat::core::ast-name node)      ;; -> String   the node's name (PARTIAL — raises)
;;   (:wat::core::ast->children node) ;; -> children
;;
;; Clojure's own pair, for dialect fidelity: (namespace 'wat.core/+) => "wat.core"
;;                                            (name 'wat.core/+)      => "+"
;;
;; Candidates:
;;   symbol-namespace  — explicit, verbose, unambiguous.
;;   sym-ns            — terse; does `ns` read as namespace here or as the `ns` FORM?
;;   namespace         — Clojure-faithful and shortest. But is a bare `namespace` too
;;                       generic at a wat call site, where FQDN spelling is the norm?
;;   ast-namespace     — matches the `ast-kind` / `ast-name` family already on the surface.
;;                       Is the symbol accessor part of that family, or its own thing?
;;
;; ★ AND THE PAIRED QUESTION: `ast-name` is PARTIAL today (it raises on a non-nameable
;;   node — the exact defect that killed the codemod on 43 of 1392 corpus files). Should the
;;   namespace accessor be TOTAL by returning the binder namespace, and does its NAME need to
;;   carry that it never fails?

(:wat::core::defn :naming::SLOT-2-namespace-accessor
  [s <- :wat::WatAST] -> :wat::core::String
  ;; <accessor-name> goes here — reads the namespace off a symbol node.
  (:wat::core::PLACEHOLDER-ACCESSOR-NAME s))

;; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; SLOT 3 — THE PREDICATE: "IS THIS SYMBOL A REFERENCE, OR A BINDER?"
;; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;;
;; The question the four `contains('/')` sites each hand-roll today. After the correction it
;; is a namespace comparison, and it wants ONE name and ONE door.
;;
;; The two directions are not symmetric — which way should the predicate read?
;;
;;   binder?      — true for the reserved namespace. Positive about the LOCAL case.
;;   bound?       — same, in $bound's register.
;;   reference?   — true for everything else. Positive about the REFERENCE case, which is
;;                  what almost every caller actually wants to know ("may I resolve this?").
;;   namespaced?  — the mechanical fact. Honest, but after the correction EVERY symbol is
;;                  namespaced, so the word would be a lie unless it means "user-namespaced".
;;   resolvable?  — what the caller does next. But it promises resolution SUCCESS, which the
;;                  predicate cannot deliver (an unknown `user/nope` is namespaced and
;;                  unresolvable) — does it over-promise?
;;
;; ★ THE HONESTY EDGE: whichever way it reads, the OPPOSITE case must not become the
;;   `_`-arm — full enum matching is doctrine here
;;   (109/NOTE-full-enum-match-mandatory-no-wildcard-arm.md), and this predicate is exactly
;;   the shape that tempts a wildcard.

(:wat::core::defn :naming::SLOT-3-role-predicate
  [s <- :wat::WatAST] -> :wat::core::bool
  ;; <predicate-name> goes here — and the NAME decides which case is the positive one.
  (:wat::core::PLACEHOLDER-PREDICATE-NAME s))

;; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; SLOT 4 — WHAT THE MISFILED NODE IS CALLED WHILE BOTH SPELLINGS LIVE
;; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;;
;; During dual-read, `:wat::core::+` and `wat.core/+` are TWO SPELLINGS OF ONE SYMBOL. The
;; record and the code currently call the first one a "keyword", which the builder's ruling
;; says is exactly the misfiling being corrected. Docs, errors and commit messages need a
;; word for it that does not re-assert the lie.
;;
;;   colon-quoted symbol  — the builder's own phrase. Says precisely what it is: a symbol
;;                          whose spelling leads with a colon. Long; is it too long for a
;;                          diagnostic?
;;   colon symbol         — terser; does dropping "quoted" lose the point?
;;   legacy spelling      — true and short, but says nothing about WHAT it is; and "legacy"
;;                          dates it rather than describing it.
;;   keyword-form symbol  — bridges to what readers have called it for months; or does
;;                          keeping the word "keyword" perpetuate the misfiling?
;;
;; ★ AND THE REAL QUESTION UNDER IT: once symbols are un-misfiled, does `WatAST::Keyword`
;;   keep its name for the VALUE role it retains, or does the variant itself want renaming
;;   so no future reader repeats the fusion its doc comment currently describes?

(:wat::core::defn :naming::SLOT-4-legacy-spelling-term
  [] -> :wat::core::String
  ;; The term a diagnostic uses for `:wat::core::+` when it must name the spelling.
  "PLACEHOLDER-LEGACY-SPELLING-TERM")
